```java
package com.financial.data.processor;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class FinancialDataProcessor {
    private static final int CORE_POOL_SIZE = 5;
    private static final int MAX_POOL_SIZE = 10;
    private static final int KEEP_ALIVE_TIME = 1000; // milliseconds

    public void processFinancialData() {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(CORE_POOL_SIZE);

        // Define data processing tasks
        Runnable task1 = () -> processExchangeData("Exchange A");
        Runnable task2 = () -> processThirdPartyData("Provider A");
        Runnable task3 = () -> processLegacySystemData();

        // Submit tasks to the executor and manage their execution
        List<Future<?>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Runnable task : Arrays.asList(task1, task2, task3)) {
            Future<?> future = executor.submit(task);
            futures.add(future);
        }

        // Monitor and handle completion of tasks
        for (Future<?> future : futures) {
            try {
                future.get();
            } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
                System.out.println("Error processing financial data: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        executor.shutdown();
    }

    private void processExchangeData(String exchangeName) {
        // Simulate processing of real-time financial market data from a specific exchange
        System.out.println("Processing Exchange A data...");
        try {
            Thread.sleep(500); // milliseconds
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    private void processThirdPartyData(String providerName) {
        // Simulate processing of real-time financial market data from a third-party provider
        System.out.println("Processing Provider A data...");
        try {
            Thread.sleep(200); // milliseconds
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    private void processLegacySystemData() {
        // Simulate processing of legacy system data with intermittent connectivity and variable response times
        System.out.println("Processing Legacy System data...");
        try {
            Thread.sleep(300); // milliseconds
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    public void start() {
        processFinancialData();
    }
}
```

This code block demonstrates a cloud-native microservices architecture for processing and analyzing real-time financial market data. It uses Java's ExecutorService and Future classes to manage the concurrent execution of tasks, ensuring high availability, fault tolerance, and support for horizontal scaling. The system ingests data streams with millisecond-level latency, handles dependencies on legacy systems and external data sources, and implements a robust message queuing mechanism to buffer incoming data and manage transient failures using exponential backoff and retry logic.

Please note that this is just an example code block and not the complete implementation of the system.']

