```java
public class TradeExecutor {
    private final Map<String, AtomicReference<Trade>> tradeMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void executeTrade(String clientId, String symbol, double quantity) throws Exception {
        try (AtomicReference<Trade> tradeRef = tradeMap.computeIfAbsent(clientId, k -> new AtomicReference<>())) {
            lock.lock();
            try {
                Trade trade = tradeRef.get();
                if (trade == null) {
                    trade = new Trade(clientId, symbol, quantity);
                    tradeRef.set(trade);
                } else {
                    // conflict resolution mechanism
                    if (!trade.symbol.equals(symbol) || !trade.quantity.equals(quantity)) {
                        throw new Exception("Duplicate trade detected");
                    }
                }

                // execute trade
                // ...

                // confirm trade
                // ...
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the trades by client ID, and an `AtomicReference` to ensure thread safety. The `ReentrantLock` is used to synchronize access to the map and trade data structures.

The code also includes basic conflict resolution logic to handle duplicate trades during high concurrency.

Note that this is just one part of a larger system, and you would need to implement other components such as the WebSocket connections, market data APIs, caching layers, logging, and alerting strategies.']

